A validator is a function that returns `null` if valid, or an error object if invalid.

```typescript
forbiddenNameValidator(nameRe: RegExp): ValidatorFn {
  return (control: AbstractControl): {[key: string]: any} | null => {
    const forbidden = nameRe.test(control.value);
    return forbidden ? { forbiddenName: { value: control.value } } : null;
  };
}
```

Then add it: `username: ['', [Validators.required, this.forbiddenNameValidator(/admin/)]]`

For server‑side checks (e.g., unique email), use async validators that return a `Promise` or `Observable`.

```typescript
emailAvailableValidator(http: HttpClient): AsyncValidatorFn {
  return (control: AbstractControl) => {
    return this.http.get(`/api/check-email?email=${control.value}`).pipe(
      map((res: any) => (res.available ? null : { emailTaken: true })),
      catchError(() => of(null))
    );
  };
}
```
Attach as third argument in the array: `email: ['', [Validators.required], [this.emailAvailableValidator]]`
